#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060796} :activity/id #uuid "3b662d58-023c-575e-8118-a4b1f65aec26" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048465 :topic/id #uuid "a1f201d2-0387-5e29-9012-9e66344cc9bc" :topic/math-academy-id 268} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060796 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060977 :tutorial/content "The unit circle provides a convenient way to represent the sine and cosine of special angles in each quadrant.\n\nWe first recall the following:\n\n- The $x$-coordinate of any point on the unit circle equals the *cosine* of the corresponding central angle, and\n- the $y$-coordinate equals the *sine* of the corresponding central angle.\n\nLet's use the unit circle to list the special values of sine and cosine, starting with the first quadrant. First, we mark off the special angles in this quadrant.\n\n![](images/3a/3a0c6eb5bc286272ef67aee5ef0fa625a6f4e44635413ca43060efc9082b1848.png)\n\nNow, let's write down each point's $x$- and $y$-coordinates, corresponding to the cosine and sine of each point's central angle. In addition, we'll highlight the $y$-coordinate at each point.\n\n![](images/68/680c69ce0313fb61addd31be65074211b866ac2dc8ceed8180c34c97169b0fc3.png)\n\nBefore we move on, observe that if we rotate counterclockwise from the positive $x$-axis, we see that the $y$-coordinates at the special angles are given by the following sequence:\n\n$$\nf(n) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt n}{2}, \\qquad n=0,1,2,3,4\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/ae/ae75322c270db13d7ee5ba6079b236b9a18d463f74cc3e5ee70b6fe113d224e3.png)\n\nThere is a similar pattern for the $x$-coordinates (corresponding to the cosine of the central angle). The only difference is that now we go in reverse, from $90^\\circ$ to $0^\\circ$, as shown below\n\n![](images/55/558f15ac17d31bb236fc63474e170ae4790baa8a9bccd375fe866998b4589258.png)\n\nTake a moment to ensure you can reproduce this diagram on your own without looking. We'll add to it shortly." :tutorial/id #uuid "307aa9da-5076-5fd6-8a9c-ad92348c8d93" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7903 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cc528712-4193-5c9b-a419-12840d9e8601" :step/math-academy-id 45528 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060797}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060797 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060978 :tutorial/content "So, we know how the unit circle can be used to list the cosine and sine of the special angles in the *first* quadrant. But what about the other quadrants?\n\nThe values in the remaining quadrants can be obtained from the coordinates in the first quadrant. Let's see how:\n\n- To obtain the points in the *second* quadrant, let's first mark off the special angles in this quadrant. These angles have the same reference angles as the angles in the first quadrant:$180^\\circ - 30^\\circ = 150^\\circ$ $180^\\circ - 45^\\circ = 135^\\circ$ $180^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 120^\\circ$ We mark these angles below and include the quadrantal angle $180^\\circ$.![](images/49/49105a62c4eecffeb32f6777ddf98022c5ca46079a33294e7eeafc9164444cba.png)\nNow, we reflect the coordinates in the first quadrant in the $y$-axis. This changes the sign of the $x$-coordinate at each point and leaves the $y$-coordinate unchanged.![](images/e4/e44980d06903b6570182b99787ca7dec8cceacab2f72dce04fc48b3d7c660059.png)\n- To obtain the points in the third quadrant, we first find the special angles in this quadrant. Again, these angles have the same reference angles as the special angles in the first quadrant:$180^\\circ + 30^\\circ = 210^\\circ$ $180^\\circ + 45^\\circ = 225^\\circ$ $180^\\circ + 60^\\circ = 240^\\circ$ Then, we take the points in the second quadrant and reflect them in the $x$-axis. This changes the sign of the $y$-coordinate at each point and leaves the $x$-coordinate unchanged.![](images/bb/bbfcf14e39ee122cf1bd7048903ac7f205325c77d4b2899c53619495ffeb8d8c.png)\n- Finally, to obtain the points in the fourth quadrant, we first compute the special angles in this quadrant:$360^\\circ - 30^\\circ = 330^\\circ$ $360^\\circ - 45^\\circ = 315^\\circ$ $360^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 300^\\circ$ Then, we take the points in the first quadrant and reflect them in the $x$-axis. This changes the sign of the $y$-coordinate at each point and leaves the $x$-coordinate unchanged.![](images/f5/f507964e8fe06cc54ceab447d570bb004b4e9a2b0b8fa3074762e28a41009a2a.png)\n\nTherefore, our complete unit circle is as follows.\n\n![](images/f5/f5006a5a3c363f83e6d4624fc72fefa140e29ef6ef23db16fbab5538b421a058.png)\n\nTake a moment to ensure you can create this diagram yourself without looking. We'll be referring to it often!" :tutorial/id #uuid "f3691d0c-59fd-5cd5-8b22-f20733d06f4a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7904 :tutorial/title "A Method For Constructing the Other Quadrants"} :step/id #uuid "d411433f-4c7a-586b-ac35-14040fe93360" :step/math-academy-id 45529 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060798}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060798 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059843 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059399 :question/id #uuid "ce1e1013-987e-5c1e-82e6-a8524d8be168" :question/math-academy-id "e-12613"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6699397d-0014-5749-b439-4a8486990986" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051043}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234462}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sine or Cosine of a Special Angle Given in Degrees"} :step/id #uuid "381d3128-fbcb-5ce6-9e0e-a3e655f68eda" :step/math-academy-id 45530 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060799}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060799 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059844 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059400 :question/id #uuid "dd543c7f-fb05-54a9-a9e3-3f7cb88940b9" :q